  ('brief_tb_2024_23_r1','tb-2024-23-turkey-and-u-s-corruption-june',1,NULL,'https://troyblackvideos.com/prophecy-archive-24/','This is a weak example. The prophecy is vague, the timeline is off, and the fulfillment feels forced to fit a pre-existing news story.','material_provisional','Falsifiability: Is it falsifiable? Barely. Too vague. Specificity: How specific was the original word? Quite low. The original wording, the claimed fulfillment, and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: Troy links it to the federal investigation into NYC Mayor Eric Adams. Subpoenas issued in July 2024; Adams was indicted in September 2024 on charges involving illegal campaign contributions from Turkey, bribery, and favors (approving a Turkish consulate despite fire code issues, avoiding statements on the Armenian Genocide). Several NYC officials resigned or were arrested in related probes. Also notes over 100 wildfires in Turkey in late July. The website entry matches the original video closely. No major additions or omissions in the core prophetic statements. Analysis: The six or seven links cover the Adams indictment, the Turkey connection, and the origin of the term "Deep State" in Turkey. ●​ The main event (Adams indictment) happened in September, not July. ●​ It''s about NYC local corruption involving Turkey, not a major "Deep State" exposure at the national U.S. level. ●​ "Fire over Turkey" is tied to literal wildfires — a stretch. ●​ No clear evidence of Turkey "making some money" as prophesied. ●​ The investigation began well before the prophecy. ●​ The Adams probe was already public knowledge before June 2024. ●​ "Deep State" is a vague, politically loaded term that can be applied to almost anything. ●​ Wildfires in Turkey are annual; linking them as prophetic "fire" is weak. Overall Verdict: This is a weak example. The prophecy is vague, the timeline is off, and the fulfillment feels forced to fit a pre-existing news story. It doesn’t hold up well under close examination. Want me to dig into another one?','Predictability: Was it humanly predictable? Yes — foreign influence investigations on politicians aren''t rare. Already public: Does he claim credit for things already public? The investigation was already underway. A reproducible prior-information search with a 2024-06-20 cutoff is still required.','This entry comes from the speaker’s “fulfilled prophecies” archive as reviewed by Joshua Harris. Because that archive selects claims described as fulfilled, it cannot by itself show the speaker’s complete track record.','["Confirm the original video URL, then have a verified human check the segment and surrounding context.", "Confirm the quoted wording against the original video; quotes here are transcribed from the independent review.", "Freeze a measurable definition of fulfillment from the original wording before any rating.", "Preserve independent outcome evidence with URLs and access dates.", "Complete a reproducible prior-information receipt with a 2024-06-20 cutoff.", "Get one named human reviewer to make and publish the final decision."]','provisional_research','2026-09-20','2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z'),
  ('brief_tb_2024_24_r1','tb-2024-24-trump-assassination-attempt-july-2024-prophecy',1,NULL,'https://troyblackvideos.com/prophecy-archive-24/','This is a classic example of a vague prophecy being applied to a major headline after it happens.','material_provisional','Falsifiability: Is it falsifiable? No, far too vague. Specificity: How specific was the original word? Very low — no names, no clear description of a shooting. The original wording, the claimed fulfillment, and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: Troy links it primarily to the July 13, 2024 Trump assassination attempt in Butler, PA (almost exactly one month later), plus the Iran plot revealed shortly after, foreign disinformation campaigns, the CrowdStrike global tech outage, and Biden dropping out of the race. The website entry matches the video closely. No significant additions or omissions in the core message. Analysis: He provides nearly ten links covering the Trump shooting, the Iran assassination plot, Russia/China/Iran spreading conflicting conspiracy theories, the CrowdStrike outage, and Biden''s withdrawal. ●​ The prophecy never mentions Trump, an assassination attempt, a shooting, or a bullet by name. ●​ "Someone from the outside" is vague and could mean many things — the shooter was a 20-year-old American, not a foreign actor. ●​ The prophecy focuses on national chaos and failed leadership/technology. The fulfillment bundles multiple unrelated July events to make it fit. ●​ Political violence against Trump had been discussed for years; threats were not uncommon. ●​ "Unprecedented events" is extremely broad — major news happens every month. ●​ Post-event, it''s easy to connect dots backward. Overall Verdict: This is a classic example of a vague prophecy being applied to a major headline after it happens. The one-month timing is the strongest element, but the lack of specific details about Trump or an assassination attempt makes the connection feel forced. It''s not one of his stronger claims.','Predictability: Was it humanly predictable? Yes — tension was high, and "something big" in July 2024 was plausible. Already public: Does he claim credit for things already public or scheduled? The core event wasn''t scheduled, but the broad framing makes it easy to attach. A reproducible prior-information search with a 2024-06-22 cutoff is still required.','This entry comes from the speaker’s “fulfilled prophecies” archive as reviewed by Joshua Harris. Because that archive selects claims described as fulfilled, it cannot by itself show the speaker’s complete track record.','["Confirm the original video URL, then have a verified human check the segment and surrounding context.", "Confirm the quoted wording against the original video; quotes here are transcribed from the independent review.", "Freeze a measurable definition of fulfillment from the original wording before any rating.", "Preserve independent outcome evidence with URLs and access dates.", "Complete a reproducible prior-information receipt with a 2024-06-22 cutoff.", "Get one named human reviewer to make and publish the final decision."]','provisional_research','2026-09-20','2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z'),
  ('brief_tb_2024_25_r1','tb-2024-25-biden-s-debate-failure-troy-black',1,NULL,'https://troyblackvideos.com/prophecy-archive-24/','This is another weak claim. The prophecy is overly vague and cryptic, and the "fulfillment" feels like a post-hoc connection to a predictable and already-discussed event.','material_provisional','Falsifiability: Is it falsifiable? No, the language is too cryptic and open to interpretation. Specificity: How specific was the original word? Extremely low — cryptic phrases with no mention of a debate, speaking, or public appearance. The original wording, the claimed fulfillment, and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: Troy connects this to Biden''s poor performance in the June 27, 2024 presidential debate against Trump, where his age, confusion, and verbal struggles were widely criticized and became a major news event. The website entry aligns closely with the video. No major differences noted. Analysis: He provides around 6–10 links covering debate reactions, media coverage of Biden''s performance, and political fallout. Troy himself says he didn''t understand at first. The debate happened literally the next day, but the wording is so vague it could apply to many situations. Biden''s age and cognitive concerns had been a major topic in media for over a year before this prophecy. The debate was a scheduled, highly anticipated event. Overall Verdict: This is another weak claim. The prophecy is overly vague and cryptic, and the "fulfillment" feels like a post-hoc connection to a predictable and already-discussed event. The timing is close, but the lack of real specificity hurts its credibility.','Predictability: Was it humanly predictable? Yes, Biden''s debate performance issues were widely anticipated. Already public: Does he claim credit for things already public? Yes, concerns about Biden''s fitness were already widespread. A reproducible prior-information search with a 2024-06-26 cutoff is still required.','This entry comes from the speaker’s “fulfilled prophecies” archive as reviewed by Joshua Harris. Because that archive selects claims described as fulfilled, it cannot by itself show the speaker’s complete track record.','["Confirm the original video URL, then have a verified human check the segment and surrounding context.", "Confirm the quoted wording against the original video; quotes here are transcribed from the independent review.", "Freeze a measurable definition of fulfillment from the original wording before any rating.", "Preserve independent outcome evidence with URLs and access dates.", "Complete a reproducible prior-information receipt with a 2024-06-26 cutoff.", "Get one named human reviewer to make and publish the final decision."]','provisional_research','2026-09-20','2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO public_research_briefs (brief_id,claim_id,revision_number,supersedes_brief_id,quotation_source_url,headline,evidence_strength,test_framing,evidence_summary,prior_information_summary,corpus_warning,missing_gates_json,research_status,as_of_date,created_at) VALUES
  ('brief_tb_2024_26_r1','tb-2024-26-second-assassination-attempt-against-trump-and',1,NULL,'https://troyblackvideos.com/prophecy-archive-24/','This is another weak claim. The prophecy is broad, uses a lot of qualifiers, and the fulfillment requires connecting distant dots.','material_provisional','Falsifiability: Is it falsifiable? No, very vague and open to interpretation. Specificity: How specific was the original word? Low — lots of hedging and possible metaphors. The original wording, the claimed fulfillment, and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: Troy links it to the September 15, 2024 assassination attempt at Trump''s golf course in West Palm Beach (5 miles from Mar-a-Lago), where Ryan Routh was spotted with a rifle, a shot was fired by Secret Service, and Trump was unharmed. For Kamala: She emerged as nominee but lost the election to Trump. He ties the "outsider/enemy" to Liz and Dick Cheney endorsing and campaigning with Harris. The website entry matches the original video. No major additions or omissions. Analysis: Nearly ten links cover the second attempt, Harris''s nomination, and Cheney endorsements. ●​ The second attempt was over two months later, not soon after the prophecy. ●​ The shooter (Routh) was not a "local boy." ●​ No actual shot was fired at Trump — Secret Service fired at the suspect. ●​ "Shot will ring out" is stretched. ●​ Kamala''s loss is tied to a very vague "emerge and drop down" phrase. ●​ Assassination attempts on Trump were already a major topic after July 13. ●​ Political endorsements and campaign losses are normal election events. ●​ The Cheney connection feels like a stretch to fit the "enemy/outsider" line. Overall Verdict: This is another weak claim. The prophecy is broad, uses a lot of qualifiers, and the fulfillment requires connecting distant dots. The second attempt timing and details don''t line up cleanly, and the Kamala Harris section feels especially forced.','Predictability: Was it humanly predictable? Yes, after the first attempt, people expected more threats. Already public: Does he claim credit for things already public? The political climate made more attempts plausible. A reproducible prior-information search with a 2024-07-22 cutoff is still required.','This entry comes from the speaker’s “fulfilled prophecies” archive as reviewed by Joshua Harris. Because that archive selects claims described as fulfilled, it cannot by itself show the speaker’s complete track record.','["Confirm the original video URL, then have a verified human check the segment and surrounding context.", "Confirm the quoted wording against the original video; quotes here are transcribed from the independent review.", "Freeze a measurable definition of fulfillment from the original wording before any rating.", "Preserve independent outcome evidence with URLs and access dates.", "Complete a reproducible prior-information receipt with a 2024-07-22 cutoff.", "Get one named human reviewer to make and publish the final decision."]','provisional_research','2026-09-20','2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z'),
  ('brief_tb_2024_27_r1','tb-2024-27-mark-cuban-s-mistake-july-29',1,NULL,'https://troyblackvideos.com/prophecy-archive-24/','This one stands out because it doesn’t even have a claimed fulfillment.','material_provisional','Falsifiability: Is it falsifiable? No — too vague, no timeline or specific asset named. Specificity: How specific was the original word? Low — only a dollar figure and “sell too early.” The original wording, the claimed fulfillment, and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: There does not appear to be a clear fulfillment claim or follow-up video tying this to any specific public event involving Mark Cuban. No news stories or links are attached showing a specific $50 million deal where he sold too early. The website entry matches the video. No major changes. Analysis: No supporting news links found for a fulfillment. The prophecy remains general with no verifiable event matching the details. Mark Cuban has publicly talked about past investment regrets (like passing on Uber early) for years before this prophecy. Vague advice like “don’t sell too early” is common business wisdom. Overall Verdict: This one stands out because it doesn’t even have a claimed fulfillment. It’s very generic advice that could apply to almost any investor. Without a specific event tied to it, it doesn’t function as a testable prophecy.','Predictability: Was it humanly predictable? Yes — generic investment advice. Already public: Does he claim credit for things already public? Not applicable here. A reproducible prior-information search with a 2024-07-29 cutoff is still required.','This entry comes from the speaker’s “fulfilled prophecies” archive as reviewed by Joshua Harris. Because that archive selects claims described as fulfilled, it cannot by itself show the speaker’s complete track record.','["Confirm the original video URL, then have a verified human check the segment and surrounding context.", "Confirm the quoted wording against the original video; quotes here are transcribed from the independent review.", "Freeze a measurable definition of fulfillment from the original wording before any rating.", "Preserve independent outcome evidence with URLs and access dates.", "Complete a reproducible prior-information receipt with a 2024-07-29 cutoff.", "Get one named human reviewer to make and publish the final decision."]','provisional_research','2026-09-20','2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z'),
  ('brief_tb_2024_28_r1','tb-2024-28-one-violent-night-august-7-2024',1,NULL,'https://troyblackvideos.com/prophecy-archive-24/','This is a weak claim. The prophecy is vague, the timing is off by a couple days, and the event was not particularly violent or unusual.','material_provisional','Falsifiability: Is it falsifiable? No, very vague. Specificity: How specific was the original word? Low — vague phrases with hedging on “violent.” The original wording, the claimed fulfillment, and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: Troy links it to early September 2024 Israel-Hamas related events: ●​ Sept 2: Large anti-Israel protest march through Manhattan (Union Square area, ~2 miles from Times Square), with flares, smoke bombs, pushing police, Hamas flags, 4 arrests. ●​ Sept 3: U.S. Justice Dept charges against Hamas leaders. ●​ Sept 4: Israeli strike in West Bank killing militants. The website matches the video closely. No major additions or omissions. Analysis: He provides several news links covering the protest, Hamas indictments, and West Bank strike. ●​ The main protest was on Sept 2, not Sept 4. ●​ It was not near Times Square. ●​ No actual “violent night” — it was a daytime march with some unruliness but not major violence. ●​ The “debate spilling into streets” is a stretch for routine protests that had been happening for months. Pro-Palestinian protests in NYC were extremely common throughout 2024. Large marches, flares, and occasional arrests were not rare events. The September 4 date doesn’t line up with the biggest activity. Overall Verdict: This is a weak claim. The prophecy is vague, the timing is off by a couple days, and the event was not particularly violent or unusual. It feels like normal protest activity being tied to a broad word after the fact.','Predictability: Was it humanly predictable? Yes, protests were ongoing. Already public: Does he claim credit for things already public? The protest pattern was already well established. A reproducible prior-information search with a 2024-08-07 cutoff is still required.','This entry comes from the speaker’s “fulfilled prophecies” archive as reviewed by Joshua Harris. Because that archive selects claims described as fulfilled, it cannot by itself show the speaker’s complete track record.','["Confirm the original video URL, then have a verified human check the segment and surrounding context.", "Confirm the quoted wording against the original video; quotes here are transcribed from the independent review.", "Freeze a measurable definition of fulfillment from the original wording before any rating.", "Preserve independent outcome evidence with URLs and access dates.", "Complete a reproducible prior-information receipt with a 2024-08-07 cutoff.", "Get one named human reviewer to make and publish the final decision."]','provisional_research','2026-09-20','2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z'),
  ('brief_tb_2024_29_r1','tb-2024-29-voice-of-star-wars-august-12',1,NULL,'https://troyblackvideos.com/prophecy-archive-24/','This is a weak claim. The prophecy is mostly a spiritual metaphor about the church, with Star Wars used symbolically.','material_provisional','Falsifiability: Is it falsifiable? No, very vague and metaphorical. Specificity: How specific was the original word? Low — mostly symbolic with no names, dates, or clear events. The original wording, the claimed fulfillment, and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: Troy links it to two events: ●​ James Earl Jones (voice of Darth Vader) died on September 9, 2024. ●​ Disney cancelled The Acolyte season 2 shortly after (announced around August 19–20). The website entry matches the video closely. No major additions or omissions. Analysis: Links cover Jones’ death, his prior AI voice rights deal with Disney, and The Acolyte’s cancellation due to poor performance. ●​ Jones was 93 and his death was not unexpected. ●​ He had already signed over AI voice rights in 2022 — two years earlier. ●​ The Acolyte cancellation happened about a week later, but the prophecy never mentions a TV show, cancellation, or specific project failing. ●​ The “sounds of Star Wars” and “album cover” imagery don’t clearly connect to either event. Star Wars fans had been complaining about Disney “ruining” the franchise for years before this prophecy. The Acolyte was already facing heavy criticism during its run. Jones’ age made his passing a statistically likely event. Overall Verdict: This is a weak claim. The prophecy is mostly a spiritual metaphor about the church, with Star Wars used symbolically. Tying it to a predictable celebrity death and a struggling show’s cancellation feels forced.','Predictability: Was it humanly predictable? Yes — ongoing fan backlash against Disney’s Star Wars and an elderly actor’s death were both foreseeable. Already public: Does he claim credit for things already public? The fan discontent was already widespread. A reproducible prior-information search with a 2024-08-12 cutoff is still required.','This entry comes from the speaker’s “fulfilled prophecies” archive as reviewed by Joshua Harris. Because that archive selects claims described as fulfilled, it cannot by itself show the speaker’s complete track record.','["Confirm the original video URL, then have a verified human check the segment and surrounding context.", "Confirm the quoted wording against the original video; quotes here are transcribed from the independent review.", "Freeze a measurable definition of fulfillment from the original wording before any rating.", "Preserve independent outcome evidence with URLs and access dates.", "Complete a reproducible prior-information receipt with a 2024-08-12 cutoff.", "Get one named human reviewer to make and publish the final decision."]','provisional_research','2026-09-20','2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z'),
  ('brief_tb_2024_30_r1','tb-2024-30-candace-owens-forced-to-take-a',1,NULL,'https://troyblackvideos.com/prophecy-archive-24/','This is one of Troy’s stronger claims. The prophecy was specific, named the person, and the event happened shortly after.','material_provisional','Falsifiability: Is it falsifiable? Yes, this one is actually fairly specific. Specificity: How specific was the original word? Quite specific — named Candace Owens and “forced to take a break.” The original wording, the claimed fulfillment, and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: Troy links it to Candace Owens being fired from The Daily Wire on August 26, 2024, after months of public tension with Ben Shapiro and the company. He presents her departure as the “forced break” that will ultimately be good for her. The website entry matches the video closely. No major additions or omissions. Analysis: He provides several news links covering her firing and the public feud with Daily Wire leadership. ●​ The conflict between Owens and The Daily Wire had been very public for months before this prophecy. ●​ Her departure was widely anticipated by many observers. This was not a surprise event. The feud had been playing out publicly on social media and in interviews for weeks. Overall Verdict: This is one of Troy’s stronger claims. The prophecy was specific, named the person, and the event happened shortly after. However, the very public nature of the feud significantly weakens its prophetic weight — many people saw this coming.','Predictability: Was it humanly predictable? Yes, very — the public feud made her exit foreseeable. Already public: Does he claim credit for things already public? Yes, the conflict was already widely known. A reproducible prior-information search with a 2024-08-15 cutoff is still required.','This entry comes from the speaker’s “fulfilled prophecies” archive as reviewed by Joshua Harris. Because that archive selects claims described as fulfilled, it cannot by itself show the speaker’s complete track record.','["Confirm the original video URL, then have a verified human check the segment and surrounding context.", "Confirm the quoted wording against the original video; quotes here are transcribed from the independent review.", "Freeze a measurable definition of fulfillment from the original wording before any rating.", "Preserve independent outcome evidence with URLs and access dates.", "Complete a reproducible prior-information receipt with a 2024-08-15 cutoff.", "Get one named human reviewer to make and publish the final decision."]','provisional_research','2026-09-20','2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z'),
  ('brief_tb_2024_31_r1','tb-2024-31-hurricane-surprise-august-19-2024',1,NULL,'https://troyblackvideos.com/prophecy-archive-24/','Troy Black Prophecy Evaluation: Hurricane Surprise (August 19, 2024) This is a weak claim.','material_provisional','Falsifiability: Is it falsifiable? Yes, this one is actually fairly specific. Specificity: How specific was the original word? Quite specific — named Candace Owens and “forced to take a break.” The original wording, the claimed fulfillment, and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: Troy links it to Hurricane Helene, which made landfall on September 26, 2024, as a Category 4 storm. It caused catastrophic damage across the Southeast, especially in western North Carolina, with many people surprised by its strength and inland flooding. The website entry matches the video closely. No major additions or omissions. The website entry matches the video closely. No major additions or omissions. Analysis: He provides several news links showing Helene’s unexpected rapid intensification and the massive damage it caused. ●​ The prophecy was given on August 19, but Helene didn’t form until September 17 — almost a month later. ●​ Hurricane season runs June to November, so predicting “a hurricane” is not specific. ●​ “Surprise” and “bigger than expected” are very common descriptions for many hurricanes. This was not a surprise event. The feud had been playing out publicly on social media and in interviews for weeks. Major hurricanes that surprise forecasters happen nearly every season. Helene was one of several strong storms in 2024. Overall Verdict (hurricane portion): Troy Black Prophecy Evaluation: Hurricane Surprise (August 19, 2024) This is a weak claim. Predicting a surprising hurricane during hurricane season is extremely low-specificity. Almost any major storm could be made to fit. The timing gap between the prophecy and the storm further weakens it.','Predictability: Was it humanly predictable? Yes, very — the public feud made her exit foreseeable. Already public: Does he claim credit for things already public? Yes, the conflict was already widely known. A reproducible prior-information search with a 2024-08-19 cutoff is still required.','This entry comes from the speaker’s “fulfilled prophecies” archive as reviewed by Joshua Harris. Because that archive selects claims described as fulfilled, it cannot by itself show the speaker’s complete track record.','["Confirm the original video URL, then have a verified human check the segment and surrounding context.", "Confirm the quoted wording against the original video; quotes here are transcribed from the independent review.", "Freeze a measurable definition of fulfillment from the original wording before any rating.", "Preserve independent outcome evidence with URLs and access dates.", "Complete a reproducible prior-information receipt with a 2024-08-19 cutoff.", "Get one named human reviewer to make and publish the final decision."]','provisional_research','2026-09-20','2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z'),
  ('brief_tb_2024_32_r1','tb-2024-32-kamala-harris-backfire-and-political-body',1,NULL,'https://troyblackvideos.com/prophecy-archive-24/','This is another weak claim. The prophecy uses very generic political language that could describe almost any failed campaign.','material_provisional','Falsifiability: Is it falsifiable? No, too vague. Specificity: How specific was the original word? Very low — generic political language. The original wording, the claimed fulfillment, and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: Troy links it to Kamala Harris losing the 2024 presidential election to Donald Trump in November. He presents her campaign’s strategy and media support as the thing that ultimately “backfired.” The website entry matches the video closely. No major additions or omissions. Analysis: He provides several links covering Harris’s election loss, polling shifts, and post-election analysis. ●​ The prophecy was given in late August, but the election was in early November — over two months later. ●​ “Backfire” and “body slam” are very vague political terms that could apply to many campaign moments. ●​ No specific event or moment is described. By August 21, Harris had already been the nominee for several weeks and was polling competitively. Election outcomes are inherently uncertain, but calling a loss “backfire” after the fact is easy to do. Overall Verdict: This is another weak claim. The prophecy uses very generic political language that could describe almost any failed campaign. There’s nothing specific enough to make this a strong prophetic word.','Predictability: Was it humanly predictable? Somewhat — elections are always uncertain. Already public: Does he claim credit for things already public? The race was already a major news story. A reproducible prior-information search with a 2024-08-21 cutoff is still required.','This entry comes from the speaker’s “fulfilled prophecies” archive as reviewed by Joshua Harris. Because that archive selects claims described as fulfilled, it cannot by itself show the speaker’s complete track record.','["Confirm the original video URL, then have a verified human check the segment and surrounding context.", "Confirm the quoted wording against the original video; quotes here are transcribed from the independent review.", "Freeze a measurable definition of fulfillment from the original wording before any rating.", "Preserve independent outcome evidence with URLs and access dates.", "Complete a reproducible prior-information receipt with a 2024-08-21 cutoff.", "Get one named human reviewer to make and publish the final decision."]','provisional_research','2026-09-20','2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z'),
  ('brief_tb_2024_33_r1','tb-2024-33-kamala-harris-two-weeks-september-9',1,NULL,'https://troyblackvideos.com/prophecy-archive-24/','This is a weak claim. Giving a two-week timeline should make it more testable, but tying a scheduled VP debate to a prophecy about Kamala Harris feels like a stretch.','material_provisional','Falsifiability: Is it falsifiable? Yes, because it gives a specific two-week timeline. Specificity: How specific was the original word? Moderately specific due to the timeline, but vague on what would happen. The original wording, the claimed fulfillment, and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: Troy links it to the September 23, 2024 Vice Presidential debate between JD Vance and Tim Walz. He claims the debate was the significant event that happened almost exactly two weeks later. The website entry matches the video closely. No major additions or omissions. Analysis: He provides links to debate coverage and reactions, claiming it was a major moment for the Harris campaign. ●​ A VP debate is a scheduled, planned political event — not an unexpected “game changer.” ●​ The debate was not widely seen as particularly significant or damaging to Harris’s campaign. ●​ The prophecy says “with Kamala Harris,” but she wasn’t even participating in the event. Political debates are regularly scheduled events. Predicting something will happen in two weeks during campaign season is not difficult. Overall Verdict: This is a weak claim. Giving a two-week timeline should make it more testable, but tying a scheduled VP debate to a prophecy about Kamala Harris feels like a stretch. The fulfillment doesn’t hold up well.','Predictability: Was it humanly predictable? Yes — a VP debate was already publicly scheduled. Already public: Does he claim credit for things already public? Yes, the debate date was already known. A reproducible prior-information search with a 2024-09-09 cutoff is still required.','This entry comes from the speaker’s “fulfilled prophecies” archive as reviewed by Joshua Harris. Because that archive selects claims described as fulfilled, it cannot by itself show the speaker’s complete track record.','["Confirm the original video URL, then have a verified human check the segment and surrounding context.", "Confirm the quoted wording against the original video; quotes here are transcribed from the independent review.", "Freeze a measurable definition of fulfillment from the original wording before any rating.", "Preserve independent outcome evidence with URLs and access dates.", "Complete a reproducible prior-information receipt with a 2024-09-09 cutoff.", "Get one named human reviewer to make and publish the final decision."]','provisional_research','2026-09-20','2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z'),
  ('brief_tb_2024_34_r1','tb-2024-34-rioter-setting-fire-to-himself-september',1,NULL,'https://troyblackvideos.com/prophecy-archive-24/','This is one of his better matches because of the unusual “setting fire to themselves” line.','material_provisional','Falsifiability: Is it falsifiable? Somewhat, due to the specific phrase. Specificity: How specific was the original word? Moderately specific on the fire part, vague on everything else. The original wording, the claimed fulfillment, and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: Troy links it to October 5, 2024, when photojournalist Samuel Mena Jr. set his left arm on fire during a pro-Palestinian protest near the White House. The protest marked the one-year anniversary of the October 7 Hamas attack. Mena was treated for burns but not seriously injured. The website entry matches the video closely. No major additions or omissions. Analysis: Links cover the incident with news from NY Post, Daily Beast, and others. ●​ This was a planned protest, not a spontaneous riot or “mass disruption.” ●​ It happened at the White House, not “the capital” in the sense of the Capitol building. ●​ Mena was in his 20s–30s, not clearly a “misguided youth.” ●​ The act was intentional self-harm as protest, not a “public stunt gone wrong.” Self-immolation and extreme protest acts have happened before. The Israel-Gaza conflict had already produced many protests by September 2024. Overall Verdict: This is one of his better matches because of the unusual “setting fire to themselves” line. However, the second paragraph of his fulfillment claim — where he says it would spark a national conversation about healthy responses and speak to the church — is completely unsupported. That discussion never happened. So while the core image partially matched, the broader meaning he attached to it was added after the fact. Still a stretch overall.','Predictability: Was it humanly predictable? Self-immolation as protest is rare but not unheard of in heated political climates. Already public: Does he claim credit for things already public? The tensions driving the protest were already well known. A reproducible prior-information search with a 2024-09-11 cutoff is still required.','This entry comes from the speaker’s “fulfilled prophecies” archive as reviewed by Joshua Harris. Because that archive selects claims described as fulfilled, it cannot by itself show the speaker’s complete track record.','["Confirm the original video URL, then have a verified human check the segment and surrounding context.", "Confirm the quoted wording against the original video; quotes here are transcribed from the independent review.", "Freeze a measurable definition of fulfillment from the original wording before any rating.", "Preserve independent outcome evidence with URLs and access dates.", "Complete a reproducible prior-information receipt with a 2024-09-11 cutoff.", "Get one named human reviewer to make and publish the final decision."]','provisional_research','2026-09-20','2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z'),
