  ('brief_tb_2024_46_r1','tb-2024-46-china-wants-war-december-4-2024',1,NULL,'https://troyblackvideos.com/prophecy-archive-24/','Very weak. This prophecy is so vague that it can never really be wrong.','preliminary_signal','Whether the December 4, 2024 word “China Wants War (December 4, 2024)” was fulfilled as spoken. The original wording and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: Troy points to continued Chinese military activity around Taiwan in 2025, U.S. intelligence reports about China’s military buildup, and diplomatic tensions. He claims these events show China’s desire for war, but no full-scale invasion occurred, proving God intervened. The website entry matches the video closely. Analysis: Links include news articles about Chinese war games near Taiwan and Pentagon reports on China’s military expansion. The links confirm ongoing tensions but show nothing dramatically new or different from previous years. ●​ Is it falsifiable? No — “China wants war but will not have it on their terms” is extremely vague and can be claimed as true whether war happens or not. ●​ Was it humanly predictable? Yes — China’s military pressure on Taiwan has been widely reported for years. ●​ Is it political commentary dressed as prophecy? Yes. ●​ Is the fulfillment retrofitted? Yes — ongoing tensions that existed long before the prophecy are being presented as fulfillment. ●​ How specific was the original word? Very low. No timeline, no specific event, just a general statement about China’s intentions. ●​ Does the fulfillment match the scale and tone? No — there was no major new development that clearly showed divine intervention stopping a war. Overall Verdict: Very weak. This prophecy is so vague that it can never really be wrong. China has been posturing around Taiwan for years. Saying “they want war but won’t get it on their terms” is not a risky or specific prediction. It’s mostly political commentary presented as prophecy.','Whether the claimed event was already public or predictable before December 4, 2024 is not yet assessed. A reproducible prior-information search with a 2024-12-04 cutoff is still required.','This entry comes from the speaker’s “fulfilled prophecies” archive as reviewed by Joshua Harris. Because that archive selects claims described as fulfilled, it cannot by itself show the speaker’s complete track record.','["Confirm the original video URL, then have a verified human check the segment and surrounding context.", "Confirm the quoted wording against the original video; quotes here are transcribed from the independent review.", "Freeze a measurable definition of fulfillment from the original wording before any rating.", "Preserve independent outcome evidence with URLs and access dates.", "Complete a reproducible prior-information receipt with a 2024-12-04 cutoff.", "Get one named human reviewer to make and publish the final decision."]','provisional_research','2026-09-20','2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z'),
  ('brief_tb_2024_47_r1','tb-2024-47-biden-s-christmas-commutations-december-11',1,NULL,'https://troyblackvideos.com/prophecy-archive-24/','Very weak. Predicting that Biden would issue controversial pardons at Christmas is not prophetic.','preliminary_signal','Whether the December 11, 2024 word “Biden''s Christmas Commutations (December 11, 2024)” was fulfilled as spoken. The original wording and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: Troy connects it to President Biden’s large-scale clemency actions in December 2024, where he commuted the sentences of nearly 1,500 people and pardoned 39 others, including several high-profile cases that sparked significant public backlash. The website entry matches the video closely. Analysis: Links go to news coverage of Biden’s December 2024 commutations and pardons, including reactions from both sides of the political aisle. ●​ Is it falsifiable? No — “pardoning people that shock the nation” is very subjective. ●​ Was it humanly predictable? Yes — it is extremely common for outgoing presidents to issue a large number of pardons and commutations during their final weeks in office, especially at Christmas time. ●​ Is it political commentary dressed as prophecy? Yes. ●​ Is the fulfillment retrofitted? Yes — he took a very predictable lame-duck action and presented it as a prophetic word. ●​ How specific was the original word? Low. No names or specific crimes were mentioned, just that it would be shocking. ●​ Does the fulfillment match the scale and tone? Partially — there was backlash, but this is standard behavior for presidents leaving office. Overall Verdict: Very weak. Predicting that Biden would issue controversial pardons at Christmas is not prophetic. This is something political observers expected to happen. Outgoing presidents have done this for decades.','Whether the claimed event was already public or predictable before December 11, 2024 is not yet assessed. A reproducible prior-information search with a 2024-12-11 cutoff is still required.','This entry comes from the speaker’s “fulfilled prophecies” archive as reviewed by Joshua Harris. Because that archive selects claims described as fulfilled, it cannot by itself show the speaker’s complete track record.','["Confirm the original video URL, then have a verified human check the segment and surrounding context.", "Confirm the quoted wording against the original video; quotes here are transcribed from the independent review.", "Freeze a measurable definition of fulfillment from the original wording before any rating.", "Preserve independent outcome evidence with URLs and access dates.", "Complete a reproducible prior-information receipt with a 2024-12-11 cutoff.", "Get one named human reviewer to make and publish the final decision."]','provisional_research','2026-09-20','2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z'),
  ('brief_tb_2024_48_r1','tb-2024-48-la-fires-prophesied-2025-december-28',1,NULL,'https://troyblackvideos.com/prophecy-archive-24/','Weak. While the timing was close and the fire was destructive, the prophecy incorrectly pointed to a Midwestern state, and major California wildfires are a recurring annual event.','preliminary_signal','Whether the December 28, 2024 word “LA Fires Prophesied 2025 (December 28, 2024)” was fulfilled as spoken. The original wording and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: Troy connects it to the major Los Angeles wildfires that began on January 1, 2025 (just four days after the prophecy), particularly the Palisades Fire. He points to the massive destruction — over 40,000 acres burned and more than 12,000 homes and structures destroyed in wealthy areas. The website entry matches the video closely. Analysis: Links cover the LA fires starting January 1, 2025, and the scale of destruction in the Pacific Palisades and surrounding areas. ●​ Is it falsifiable? No — wildfires happen every year in California. ●​ Was it humanly predictable? Yes — California has a well-known wildfire season, and Los Angeles-area fires are common. ●​ Is it political commentary dressed as prophecy? No, but it’s disaster commentary. ●​ Is the fulfillment retrofitted? Yes — he strongly suggested a Midwestern state, but the fulfillment is in California. ●​ Does he claim credit for things already public? The risk of California wildfires in early 2025 was widely understood. ●​ How specific was the original word? Low to moderate — the visual is decent, but the location guess was wrong. ●​ Does the fulfillment match the scale and tone? The fire event was significant, but the Midwestern detail and some of the other phrases don’t line up. ●​ Did he emphasize the accurate parts and quietly drop the inaccurate ones? Yes — the Midwestern impression is largely ignored in the fulfillment claim. Overall Verdict: Weak. While the timing was close and the fire was destructive, the prophecy incorrectly pointed to a Midwestern state, and major California wildfires are a recurring annual event. The fulfillment requires overlooking the location mismatch.','Whether the claimed event was already public or predictable before December 28, 2024 is not yet assessed. A reproducible prior-information search with a 2024-12-28 cutoff is still required.','This entry comes from the speaker’s “fulfilled prophecies” archive as reviewed by Joshua Harris. Because that archive selects claims described as fulfilled, it cannot by itself show the speaker’s complete track record.','["Confirm the original video URL, then have a verified human check the segment and surrounding context.", "Confirm the quoted wording against the original video; quotes here are transcribed from the independent review.", "Freeze a measurable definition of fulfillment from the original wording before any rating.", "Preserve independent outcome evidence with URLs and access dates.", "Complete a reproducible prior-information receipt with a 2024-12-28 cutoff.", "Get one named human reviewer to make and publish the final decision."]','provisional_research','2026-09-20','2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z'),
  ('brief_tb_2024_49_r1','tb-2024-49-china-hacks-u-s-treasury-and',1,NULL,'https://troyblackvideos.com/prophecy-archive-24/','Weak to moderate. The same-day Treasury hack timing looks impressive at first glance, but the original prophecy was about financial systems going haywire and U.S.','preliminary_signal','Whether the December 30, 2024 word “China Hacks U.S. Treasury and China Trade War (December” was fulfilled as spoken. The original wording and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: Troy claims two fulfillments: ●​ The same day he posted the video (Dec 30, 2024), news broke that Chinese hackers breached the U.S. Treasury Department and stole documents (a “major incident”). ●​ In 2025, Trump’s tariffs sparked a nearly year-long trade war with China that caused money systems to “go haywire,” with market instability, price increases, and economic ripple effects. The website entry matches the video. It explicitly connects the prophecy to both the China Treasury hack on the same day and the 2025 U.S.-China trade war. Analysis: Links cover the Dec 30, 2024 Treasury hack news and 2025 tariff/trade war coverage. The hack timing is striking, but the original prophecy focused on financial systems/money machines, not a cyber breach of the Treasury. ●​ Is it falsifiable? No — too vague on the exact events. ●​ Was it humanly predictable? Yes — U.S.-China economic tensions and cyber attacks were already ongoing. ●​ Is it political commentary dressed as prophecy? Yes. ●​ Is the fulfillment retrofitted? Yes — he’s combining two separate 2025 events (a cyber hack and a trade war) to fit a vague financial/China word. ●​ Does he claim credit for things already public? The Treasury hack was announced the same day he posted, but tensions with China were long-standing. ●​ How specific was the original word? Low — “money machines go haywire” and “China/dragon” is broad enough to attach to many things. ●​ Does the fulfillment match the scale and tone? The trade war part is a stretch; the hack timing is the strongest element but doesn’t match the “money machines” imagery well. Overall Verdict: Weak to moderate. The same-day Treasury hack timing looks impressive at first glance, but the original prophecy was about financial systems going haywire and U.S. leaders looking to China for debt relief — not a cyber breach. The trade war connection feels even more forced. The vagueness allows him to claim multiple unrelated events as fulfillment.','Whether the claimed event was already public or predictable before December 30, 2024 is not yet assessed. A reproducible prior-information search with a 2024-12-30 cutoff is still required.','This entry comes from the speaker’s “fulfilled prophecies” archive as reviewed by Joshua Harris. Because that archive selects claims described as fulfilled, it cannot by itself show the speaker’s complete track record.','["Confirm the original video URL, then have a verified human check the segment and surrounding context.", "Confirm the quoted wording against the original video; quotes here are transcribed from the independent review.", "Freeze a measurable definition of fulfillment from the original wording before any rating.", "Preserve independent outcome evidence with URLs and access dates.", "Complete a reproducible prior-information receipt with a 2024-12-30 cutoff.", "Get one named human reviewer to make and publish the final decision."]','provisional_research','2026-09-20','2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z'),
  ('brief_tb_2025_01_r1','tb-2025-01-u-k-and-u-s-february',1,NULL,'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5n8QpgQXXaU','Weak to moderate on timing and topic. Weak as a fulfilled prediction.','material_provisional','Falsifiability: Is it falsifiable? Only weakly. “This February” is testable. Almost every other line is metaphor or was pre-softened (“war of words,” “could be a trump card,” “U.K. or U.S.,” “we’ll understand later”). Hard to fail. Specificity: How specific was the original word? Medium on calendar (February) and geography (U.S. + U.K.). Low on events. No protest, no Apple, no Pentagon, no tariffs, no named persons except the ambiguous “trump.” The original wording, the claimed fulfillment, and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: The Feb 5 livestream opens with a review of the February word. Troy treats it as already landing. Follow-up emphasis: • Pentagon removing NBC and other legacy outlets from dedicated workstations = looking glass / media crashing down • Feb 1 London Tommy Robinson march, MEGA hats, “we want our country back,” Musk support = rumbling felt around the globe, and a war of words • The “merciless march” is linked to a U.K. street march inspired by Trump, even though Trump himself did not march Website fulfillment then stacks additional February-March items (Apple spy order, tariff fight, Politico/embassy subscription cuts) that the Feb 5 follow-up only partly covered. Net claim: February 2025 U.S.-U.K. political fighting + media shakeup + U.K. privacy/speech conflict = the prophecy. The original word is a February-dated symbolic cluster: U.S. shaking felt globally, a crashing mirror, the U.K. looking foolish, some kind of “war,” and Trump on a cleanup rampage. After inauguration, the archive pointed at real February stories that share some of those nouns. That is not the same as a specific prediction coming true. The strongest original elements (a month lock, U.S. + U.K., media-as-mirror, Trump acting aggressively) are also the most predictable elements of early Trump II. The distinctive phrases – valiant warriors falling, motherland starting a war, states in the union rumbling, a merciless march, the ruckus ending after a trumpet blast – are either redefined or left unmatched. This… Analysis: The linked stories are real: • Large Feb 1, 2025 London demonstration around Tommy Robinson • Trump tariff comments toward Europe/U.K. in early February 2025 • Feb 7, 2025 reporting that the U.K. had ordered Apple to allow access to encrypted iCloud data • Early-February Pentagon press-pool changes and later cuts to government news subscriptions Discrepancies: • Timing: The word is “this February.” Several headline pieces the archive uses either started in 2024 (U.K. speech prosecutions; Apple order issued in January) or completed in March (tariffs on the U.K.). • “States in the union”: Original language points at the United States. Fulfillment leans on a London protest plus Washington media staffing. • “Start a war” / “motherland”: No war started. The archive accepts Troy’s own pre-event hedge that it might only be a war of words. • “Valiant warriors” brought down: Never matched to named people who actually fell in February. • “Trump will blast and put an end to this ruckus”: The month did not end the ruckus. Tariff and media fights continued. • “Merciless march to clean this up”: Mapped onto a protest Trump did not lead, or onto a general aggressive communications/tariff posture… Overall verdict Weak to moderate on timing and topic. Weak as a fulfilled prediction. February + U.S./U.K. + Trump-vs-media is the best part of the word, and those themes did show up. That is not surprising eleven days before a Trump inauguration. The archive then stitches several separate February stories onto metaphors that were never specified in advance. Scale, unique details, and the “war / warriors / march / ruckus ends” lines do not hold. Not a miss so complete that the month was empty. Not a hit specific enough to count as strong evidence of supernatural foresight.','Predictability: Was it humanly predictable? Yes. January 2025, pre-inauguration: Trump vs. media, tariffs on Europe, U.K. migration/speech fight, MAGA-style U.K. protest energy. Analysts were already writing that script. Already public: Does he claim credit for things already public or scheduled? Partly. Inauguration was set. Tariff threats and media confrontation were campaign promises. U.K. speech/Robinson controversy was already running. The Apple demand existed before February reporting. A reproducible prior-information search with a 2025-01-09 cutoff is still required.','This entry comes from the speaker’s “fulfilled prophecies” archive as reviewed by Joshua Harris. Because that archive selects claims described as fulfilled, it cannot by itself show the speaker’s complete track record.','["Have a verified human check the original-video segment and surrounding context.", "Confirm the quoted wording against the original video; quotes here are transcribed from the independent review.", "Freeze a measurable definition of fulfillment from the original wording before any rating.", "Preserve independent outcome evidence with URLs and access dates.", "Complete a reproducible prior-information receipt with a 2025-01-09 cutoff.", "Get one named human reviewer to make and publish the final decision."]','provisional_research','2026-09-20','2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO public_research_briefs (brief_id,claim_id,revision_number,supersedes_brief_id,quotation_source_url,headline,evidence_strength,test_framing,evidence_summary,prior_information_summary,corpus_warning,missing_gates_json,research_status,as_of_date,created_at) VALUES
  ('brief_tb_2025_02_r1','tb-2025-02-year-and-a-half-malaysia-prophecy',1,NULL,'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iK0EfKXWkBs','Weak to moderate regional resonance. Weak as a single fulfilled word.','material_provisional','Falsifiability: Falsifiable? Barely. Eighteen months, multiple countries, natural or man-made. Specificity: Specificity? Medium on region (Malaysia-ish) and actor (Trump). Low on event type and date. The original wording, the claimed fulfillment, and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: Over ~18 months, ASEAN-area cooperation plus Trump diplomacy around a Thailand-Cambodia fight, plus Myanmar quake/refugees/boats, plus some haze/cyclone cooperation = the word. Some images can be lined up if you allow two storylines: smoke/eruption ~ war or quake or haze; boats ~ Myanmar refugees; umbrella/harmony ~ ASEAN; moth ~ Trump call. That is mosaic matching across a long window, not one forecast hitting one event. The archive itself splits the images across different crises. Analysis: Real events: Thai-Cambodia 2025 clashes; Trump/ASEAN involvement; Myanmar quake; long- running Rohingya/Myanmar boat traffic toward Malaysia. Gaps: - Malaysia is the labeled country. The main “eruption” assigned is a mainland border war between two neighbors, justified because Malaysia chaired ASEAN that year. - “Force of nature” and boats are missing from the war story, so a second crisis is stapled on. - Myanmar boat crossings and ASEAN politics did not start in 2025; the 2021 coup and years of sea crossings were already the baseline. - 18 months in one of the world’s busiest disaster/diplomacy regions is a wide net. - “Eruption of sorts” is fulfilled by a journalist’s verb (“erupted”). - Trump “stepping in” fits his public deal-broker style; it is not a unique, named action given in advance. Verdict: Weak to moderate regional resonance. Weak as a single fulfilled word. Long window + split storylines.','Predictability: Humanly predictable? Partly. ASEAN chair year, Myanmar war/refugees, Trump as deal-maker were known. Already public: Already public? Myanmar boats, 2021 coup isolation from ASEAN, seasonal haze were ongoing. A reproducible prior-information search with a 2025-01-15 cutoff is still required.','This entry comes from the speaker’s “fulfilled prophecies” archive as reviewed by Joshua Harris. Because that archive selects claims described as fulfilled, it cannot by itself show the speaker’s complete track record.','["Have a verified human check the original-video segment and surrounding context.", "Confirm the quoted wording against the original video; quotes here are transcribed from the independent review.", "Freeze a measurable definition of fulfillment from the original wording before any rating.", "Preserve independent outcome evidence with URLs and access dates.", "Complete a reproducible prior-information receipt with a 2025-01-15 cutoff.", "Get one named human reviewer to make and publish the final decision."]','provisional_research','2026-09-20','2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z'),
  ('brief_tb_2025_03_r1','tb-2025-03-two-tech-companies-combining',1,NULL,'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s5APHYNS_SY','Weak. Correct category (tech combination + announcement). Tech-merger talk in early 2025 was constant (AI roll-ups, Musk empire consolidation).','material_provisional','Falsifiability: Falsifiable? Only as “some tech merger sometime.” Weak. Specificity: Specificity? Low. No names, date, or sector beyond “tech.” The original wording, the claimed fulfillment, and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: Date shared: January 16, 2025 Original: https://www.youtube.com/live/s5APHYNS_SY Original claims Vision: two bundled, frayed groups of wires fuse into one. Heard: “Two tech companies joining forces and merging together to form an alliance.” “Industry leaders.” “Major announcement.” “There’s going to be some political persuasion attached to this. Some ‘outside help’ helping them to make this decision.” No company names, no date. Website vs original Quotes match. Fulfillment added: March 28, 2025, Elon Musk announces xAI acquires X (all-stock; xAI ~$80B, X ~$33B). “Political persuasion” is tied to Musk’s Trump-advisor role and his OpenAI feud / Feb 2025 buyout attempt. Fulfillment and discrepancies The announcement happened. Problems: • X and xAI were already both Musk companies. This is internal combination, not two independent industry leaders forming an alliance under outside pressure. • Grok was already trained on X data; integration was the public direction for two years. • “Outside help / political persuasion” is not shown as a third party forcing the deal. The archive points at Musk’s own politics. • No timeline in the original; two months later is convenient, not specified. Other stones Tech-merger talk in early 2025 was constant (AI roll-ups, Musk empire consolidation). A nameless “two tech companies will announce a merger” word has a high base rate. 10 questions 1. Falsifiable? Only as “some tech merger sometime.” Weak. 2. Predictable? Yes for Musk/X/xAI specifically… Analysis: Tech-merger talk in early 2025 was constant (AI roll-ups, Musk empire consolidation). A nameless “two tech companies will announce a merger” word has a high base rate. Verdict: Weak. Correct category (tech combination + announcement). Wrong implied structure (two separate firms + outside political pressure). Highly predictable inside Musk’s own stack.','Predictability: Predictable? Yes for Musk/X/xAI specifically; generic merger even more so. Already public: Already public? The intertwining of X and xAI was already stated strategy. A reproducible prior-information search with a 2025-01-16 cutoff is still required.','This entry comes from the speaker’s “fulfilled prophecies” archive as reviewed by Joshua Harris. Because that archive selects claims described as fulfilled, it cannot by itself show the speaker’s complete track record.','["Have a verified human check the original-video segment and surrounding context.", "Confirm the quoted wording against the original video; quotes here are transcribed from the independent review.", "Freeze a measurable definition of fulfillment from the original wording before any rating.", "Preserve independent outcome evidence with URLs and access dates.", "Complete a reproducible prior-information receipt with a 2025-01-16 cutoff.", "Get one named human reviewer to make and publish the final decision."]','provisional_research','2026-09-20','2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z'),
  ('brief_tb_2025_04_r1','tb-2025-04-sesame-street',1,NULL,'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ctelqah5eBM','Weak. Named brand plus a pre-existing story that later got louder.','material_provisional','Falsifiability: Falsifiable? No. “Something Sesame-related changes / upsets people” is wide. Specificity: Specificity? Names the property (Sesame Street). Event details are poetic and vague. The original wording, the claimed fulfillment, and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: Date shared: January 18, 2025 Original: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ctelqah5eBM Related page: https://troyblackvideos.com/god-warned-me-whats-coming-for-these-beloved- characters/ Original claims Vision of Big Bird and other Sesame Street characters. Heard: “Turning a new leaf. Closing a chapter in history. Closing one door and opening another slightly. A work of ‘magic’ finally undone, transposed onto something new.” “They are going to start a new season and open up a new can of worms. . . something new and un-resounding in the hearts of the audience.” “The traditional audience is dead. ‘Let’s move on,’ so they say.” Website vs original Quotes match. Fulfillment: early February 2025 viral anger that USAID had authorized about $20 million for an Iraqi Sesame Street-style show. Trump Jr. post, Sen. Chris Coons defending it on CNN Feb 8. Archive itself notes: Breitbart reported the Iraqi show on December 23, 2024 – before Troy’s Jan 18 video. It says the story only “went viral” after the prophecy. Discrepancies • The program was already public. • Original images are about the show/characters/audience/new season. Fulfillment is a U.S. spending-outrage cycle, not Sesame Workshop closing a chapter or launching a controversial new U.S. season. • “Can of worms” is stretched to cable-news mockery of foreign aid. Other stones DOGE/USAID waste hunting was the defining week-one Trump/Musk project. Any odd USAID line item was going to trend. This one happened to involve Sesame Stree… Analysis: DOGE/USAID waste hunting was the defining week-one Trump/Musk project. Any odd USAID line item was going to trend. This one happened to involve Sesame Street. Verdict: Weak. Named brand plus a pre-existing story that later got louder. Not a clean hit.','Predictability: Predictable? The specific grant story was already published. Already public: Already public? Yes – Dec 23, 2024 Breitbart piece. A reproducible prior-information search with a 2025-01-18 cutoff is still required.','This entry comes from the speaker’s “fulfilled prophecies” archive as reviewed by Joshua Harris. Because that archive selects claims described as fulfilled, it cannot by itself show the speaker’s complete track record.','["Have a verified human check the original-video segment and surrounding context.", "Confirm the quoted wording against the original video; quotes here are transcribed from the independent review.", "Freeze a measurable definition of fulfillment from the original wording before any rating.", "Preserve independent outcome evidence with URLs and access dates.", "Complete a reproducible prior-information receipt with a 2025-01-18 cutoff.", "Get one named human reviewer to make and publish the final decision."]','provisional_research','2026-09-20','2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z'),
  ('brief_tb_2025_05_r1','tb-2025-05-one-week-prophecy-government-rats-exposed',1,NULL,'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Kaz4FqvfG5U','Moderate timing, weak uniqueness. One of the tighter short windows in the early-2025 set, but it rides a fully public purge.','material_provisional','Falsifiability: Falsifiable? Partly, because of the ~8-day window. Specificity: Specificity? Medium on timing. Low on agency, names, or the hotel story. The original wording, the claimed fulfillment, and the measure of a hit must be frozen from the original video before any rating.','Independent review by Joshua Harris (September 2026). Archive claim: Date shared: January 31, 2025 Original: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Kaz4FqvfG5U Original claims • “There’s a rat underneath the sink right now, and in the toilets.” Impression: government buildings / government as a house. • “Making changes behind closed doors. . . but he’s about to get caught.” Rat in a trap. • “I’m putting an eight day timeline on this.” • “An uncovering. A general. A major undertaking. A major deal. He’s getting uncovered right at this moment.” Surfaces next week. • “There’s a rat in hiding.” Then “she” language: “She’s getting uncovered, and restitution will be paid back to those she took advantage of.” Impression: “she” = a movement, not only one person. • Personal story: he once caught five rats in one night. “Concerted effort. . . swiftly and quickly.” Website vs original Quotes match. Fulfillment: Feb 10 Musk flags FEMA using ~$59 million in disaster money for NYC migrant hotels; Feb 11 DHS fires FEMA CFO Mary Comans and three other FEMA staff; same day Trump fires USAID Inspector General Paul Martin after a critical report. Archive notes five people let go vs the five-rats story. Discrepancies • Strict 8 days from Jan 31 is Feb 8. Firings are Feb 11. Archive says “a little over a week.” • “She” / movement is mapped onto “deep state activists” language in a DHS statement. • “A general” is unused or loosely stret